{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789855441,"nonce":"mxr6JZwfaDhQS2/mge8Ws6Cj9+A=","title":"Interpreter crashes intermittently on the whole unit suite: a live object's line is recycled","message":"`ash --mode interp bin/unit.hl` (Haxe 4.3.6 unit suite, whole-suite run,\nnot the per-case harness) dies with SIGSEGV in roughly one run in ten on\nthe NUC: `op_field_get` on a null object, or `hlp_get_obj_rt` reading\n`t-\u003eobj` of a type with no object (a vdynamic box where an object was\nexpected). Present before today's GC work: 922dda3 crashed 5/48.\n\nWhat is established (each line is 24-72 runs on the NUC):\n- `ASH_GC_RECYCLE=0`: 0 crashes. `ASH_GC_TLAB=0`: 0. `ASH_GC_TRIGGER_MB=2048`\n  (almost no collections): 0. So a collection at a bump-region refill,\n  followed by a recycled line run, is the mechanism.\n- `ASH_GC_QUARANTINE=1 ASH_GC_POISON=1` (freed blocks never reused, poisoned):\n  same crash, same fault addresses -- whole-block reuse is not involved,\n  the reused memory is a recycled line in a kept block.\n- `ASH_GC_STRESS=1`: 0 crashes, because stress turns the TLAB off and\n  interpreter-mode collections then defer to snapshot points; the refill\n  path is the only one that collects mid-native in interp mode.\n- Serial marking (`ASH_GC_MARK_THREADS=1`): still crashes. Kinds\n  unsegregated, and minors off (`ASH_GC_GEN=0`): still crashes. Scanning\n  pointer-free blocks anyway: still crashes.\n- Replaying the crashing run's collection points with\n  `ASH_GC_COLLECT_AT_REFILLS` does not reproduce it: the allocation sequence\n  is not deterministic across runs, because the sweep walked `used_blocks`\n  (a HashSet) in hash order and the recycle spans were handed out in that\n  order.\n- Sorting the sweep's block walk by address (in 4659c30's successor): with\n  minors off, 0/72. With minors on (`ASH_GC_GEN=1`), 4/24. Both the sweep\n  audit (`ASH_GC_SWEEP_AUDIT=1`, now covering recycled lines and decoding\n  NaN-boxed roots) and gdb change the timing enough that the crash never\n  shows under them, and the audit never reported a root into a recycled\n  run.\n\nSo: the address-ordered sweep is shipped (it is deterministic and better\nfor locality anyway) and it makes the majors-only collector clean on this\nsuite at 0/72, but the mechanism is not understood, and the generational\nmode still hits it, so it is opt-in until this is closed.\n\nWhere to look next: the refill collection lands inside a native holding a\nGC pointer somewhere the collector cannot see (the conservative stack scan\ncovers Rust locals on the native stack; a `Vec` does not), or the sweep\nhands out a run that a live object still occupies; the audit says the\nlatter never happens with the audit on. A deterministic reproduction needs\nthe allocation sequence pinned first: with the sorted sweep,\n`ASH_GC_COLLECT_AT_REFILLS` from a crashing run's `ASH_GC_STATS` output\n(`refill=` on each `[gc] #n` line) should now replay it.","files":null}]}